I've had a lot more trouble finding this kind of information on the Web than
I expected to have.

I have a Digital Unix v4.0d system on a Digital AlphaServer which
Digital/Compaq has certified as entirely compliant, but because of onerous
and absurd bureaucratic requirements from some higher-ups, I have to do and
document an extensive battery of tests.  Unfortunately we are a small
department which only has the one box and certainly couldn't buy another one
to do testing on, so it'll have to be on my production system.  Yes, I know
the tests are more dangerous than Y2K itself, but believe me, there's no
flexibility on this.

Running on this system is our own software, Informix SE, QMaster, and a
handful of other programs, each of which is already certified as compliant
from the vendor.

In order to complete the documentation I'll have to set dates to each of
about two dozen key dates, and also set to just before each of those dates
and roll forward to them (with power on and with power off).  Some of the
dates are quite far in the future (including one in 2027).

I don't anticipate problems with the roll-forward, except possibly that some
programs might object about licenses that have expired and refuse to run.
I'm less sure about the rollback.  I'll be working in a test copy of our
database, of course, but what effects can I expect on the file system?  What
steps do I have to take beforehand (other than the inevitable pair of full
backups that I hope to not have to use) to prepare?  What will I need to do
to recover?  What are the gotchas?

References to any URLs or other sources of information on this would be
appreciated as well.  (But please don't try to sell me your consultant
services or your special testing software.  Unless you're willing to provide
them for the amount in my Y2K testing budget -- which in honor of the Y2K
problem itself, got rolled over to $00.)

