We just completed a Y2k test exercise on DEC UNIX 4.0d with Oracle,
GIS, SAS, and a bunch of other stuff. My solution to the
roll-forward/roll-back problems was to perform a full level-zero dump
of all file systems while booted from CD. This got me an absolutely
clean full backup of the system prior to the testing.

After the backup, we tested Y2k stuff to our hearts content. When we
finished the testing, I wiped the partitions with newfs and restored
from my backups. The end results: as far as the system (and databases)
were concerned, the testing never happened -- all files were just as
users had left them prior to the testing.

The downside to doing it this way is it's considerably more work on
the sys admin's part. It ain't too easy getting things to work
properly to perform the backup and restore when booted in
single-user-mode from the CD. Plus, it ruined a weekend. But, I
wouldn't do it any other way.

BTW, we had no problems with any of our applications or UNIX while we
were in the next century.

Dave Thompson
Systems Administration Supervisor


