al012 wrote in message <375F0E88.51FB9FB5@energex.com.au>...
>When I was doing my sys admin course in Oracle. I was told that
the
>only way to do an online backup was to put the Oracle database
in
>a certain mode and then copy the data to tape. Recently I have
>been looking at cloning fileset in AdvFS. Why can't I simply
clone
>an existing fileset containing an oracle database and write
>the clone to tape? That way the clone is almost like a snapshot
>of the oracle database?

What about in memory buffers, what about data that changes during
the cloning. The issues are the same, it's just that a clone is
quicker to make. So the problems are less likely, but still
there.

Whatever you do you need to
1) find or create a point in time where the database is
internally consistent
2) divert changes to the database to a buffer area
3) copy the data elsewhere(clone disk, tape, whatever)
4) process the changes buffered in 2
5) revert back to normal operation

The advantage of AdVFS clones is that step 3 can be short.

If you do things without letting the database play a part, then
your system is going to need to recover at startup in much the
same way (from the database point of view) that it would if you
cut the power, and then switched back on. This should work, but
you rely on Oracle Corp's disaster recovery techniques, not on
their backup procedures.


Just my thoughts
Adam






